    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="default-lt" TIER_ID="default"/>
    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="imported-sep" TIER_ID="SPEAKER_00">
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a1"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts1" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts6">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>We're going to talk about organizational ethics and why they're so important. And to do that, we have a very special guest, Dr. Ryan Beisel. So let's get into the details.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a2"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts9" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts12">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Hey there, Alex Lyon. Welcome back to the channel. Today we have a very special guest with us, Dr. Ryan Beisel. He is a professor at the University of Oklahoma. He studies organizational communication and also has a special focus on leadership, morals, and ethics. And so he is the perfect person to come and talk to us about this topic, organizational ethics. And in fact, he wrote a book about organizational moral learning. And this is actually the</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a3"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts14" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts16">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Second video in a two-part series. In that first video, we talked about what are organizational ethics and morals and looking at comparing and contrasting the similarities and differences. So, I encourage you to go take a look at that video when you're done with this one. I will put a link to that in the description below. And you can find his book.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a4"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts18" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts20">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Book in the description below. Feel free to check it out. So, Ryan, welcome back to the channel. Good to have you on again. Thanks, Alex. Good to be back.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a5"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts22" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts24">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>I'm really excited today because we're just getting deeper and deeper into your particular area of expertise. So let's start with a big question. What are organizational ethics?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a8"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts38" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts40">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>And so why do organizational ethics matter?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a10"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts46" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts48">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, I love that. They're just the right thing to do. I have an expression that I talk about in workshops and also in my college classes is that good ethics is good business. They're one and the same. When organizations are doing unethical things, it harms their organization. It harms their bottom line. And when they're acting well, when they're behaving ethically, it helps their business grow and thrive. So they're one and the same. It's not like, oh, ethics are off to the side. It's central to what organizations do. But nevertheless, as you mentioned, they don't always do well. It seems like organizations are always in the news. Their leaders are often in the news for doing and getting caught doing unethical things, even criminal activity. So what is the research in organizational communication?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a11"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts50" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts52">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>offer, what kind of explanations do we have for that?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a15"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts70" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts72">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, really hard to talk about. Much easier to talk about, oh, we lost money last quarter.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a16"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts74" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts76">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>You know, much harder to talk about, hey, that was a moral failing on your part.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a17"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts78" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts80">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So how do we know that you get very</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a19"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts84" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts88">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, how do I talk about my own problems? So it's super interesting research. You know, I've read some of your research, but how do we know the moral mum effect is common in organizations?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a24"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts110" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts112">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, really hard to talk about those kinds of issues, even when we know personally, that's not ethical. That's not the right way to go. But how do I say something about it? It's like we lack the vocabulary. So what's the big deal with the moral mum effect?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a26"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts118" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts120">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, huge. They grow in the dark, right? They become extremely harmful, extremely expensive to rectify when we don't address them early. And then by the time it eventually becomes public knowledge, which it almost always does, it's very hard to keep, you know, very hard nowadays to keep any secrets.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a27"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts122" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts124">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>And especially in corporations, people will post things on social media eventually and it'll get out and then you have to pay, you know, sometimes ten times the amount of money, it's caused ten times the amount of harm. We've seen case after case in the news where people knew about this stuff.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a28"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts126" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts128">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes, that's a common theme.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a30"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts135" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts136">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So when they know, let's transition now to these five steps that I want to talk about, that you want to talk about. And that is, what can we do to combat the moral mom effect? You have five steps, so let's walk through them. Number one, speak by example. Tell us more about that.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a32"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts142" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts144">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So you use those labels, which is funny because we're told, oh, labels are bad. But when you see something unethical, something immoral, it's a moment where you pop that bubble of silence and you say, that's wrong. And then everybody goes, hey, look at the world didn't end. Someone said it was wrong. And now, like you said, now it's discussable. We can talk about right and wrong. Number two. Number two, avoid excessively philosophical language. Tell us about that.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a35"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts154" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts156">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Great. Number three, talk about what the body knows. What does that mean?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a37"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts162" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts164">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, we're going to feel it before we have the words, right? Yes, we will.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a38"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts166" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts168">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>I'm not going to say what it was. I remember years ago, you know, I saw something happen at work and I felt it like, oh, that's not right. But there's a distance there between feeling it and knowing in my body that it was not cool what I was witnessing and being able to talk about it. But you got to, you know, you got to trust that feeling that, look, that's real. What you saw wasn't right. And that's the beginning of being able to have that conversation.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a39"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts170" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts172">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Number four, retell stories of heroes who spoke up. Tell us about that.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a41"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts178" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts180">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, great. And then they hear that other people have done it who have come before them, and it makes the path forward much more clear for them when those heroes are being held up as the people to look up to and emulate. And number five, build trusting relationships. Can you explain that?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a43"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts186" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts192">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Wander full dozer 5 steps to have the hard conversations about ethical questions, unethical activities, something that you might notice and not know how to begin that conversation. These five approaches are excellent. So, thank you for unpacking that.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a44"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts196" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts198">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So, Ryan, thanks again for being on the channel. And I want to remind everybody that Ryan does have this book, Organizational Moral Learning, and that is available on Amazon. You can find the link in the description below. You feel free to check that out. Ryan, thanks for being on, man. Thank you. </ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a140"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts205" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts206">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>And until the next time, we'll see you all later. Take care.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
    </TIER>
