    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="default-lt" TIER_ID="SPEAKER_01">
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a146"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts27" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts28">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes, great. So let's just start with ethics first.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a147"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts29" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts32">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>They're widely shared standards of right conduct and so
[01:36.780 --&gt; 01:43.340]  organizational ethics then is widely shared standards of right conduct in the workplace setting and of course</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a148"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts35" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts36">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Ethics then is widely shared standards of right conduct in the workplace setting. And of course, a very important topic.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a149"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts43" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts45">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, organizational ethics matter because without standards of right conduct, organizations can hurt people, their employees and customers, the environment as well. That list goes on and on. You know, without strong organizational ethics, of course, organizations run the risk of hurting their reputations, not just people, but hurting themselves. I mean, fact of the matter is, with poor organizational ethics, organizations run the risks of undoing themselves, downright failing. A classic example of this, of course, would be Enron during the 2000s. Enron was doing a lot of unscrupulous accounting practices, which were eventually caught. And of course, Enron eventually folded, and lots of people were devastated because of it. Just a really sad story. You know, furthermore, it's not just that bad ethics hurt, it's that good ethics help. You know, there's some fascinating studies of the fact that, at least in the long run, you might say, virtuous organizations do tend to do quite well. A study right after 9-11 of some of the U.S. airlines, of course, people weren't flying as much after 9-11. There was some constriction of the airline industry. And there was a study of how airline industry companies handled that. And what you find is where airlines did the right thing, where they were generous and compassionate and transparent with employees. And 12 months later, they were doing well. A lot of airlines were not compassionate and transparent. And as a matter of fact, you saw that in their profits a year later. You saw it harm their profits. Now, I think it's important also when we talk about why do ethics matter. I mean, ethics matter also just say that they matter because they matter. It matters because doing the right thing is important, full stop. We can do it just for its own good, and we're right to do so.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a150"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts55" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts57">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, great question. It's absolutely true. As we read the news, we just see organizations behaving badly, even criminally, all over the place. And organizational communication research provides some interesting insights into why that might be. Now, one idea is that there exists what's called a moral mum effect, a moral mum effect in the workplace. Now, a moral mummy,</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a151"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts61" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts65">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Basically, it refers to this idea that organizational members have a tendency to avoid labeling unethical behavior as unethical. They might privately have.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a152"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts67" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts69">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Concerns, but they don't tend to publicly discuss those concerns. And that can kick off a series of other problematic communication dynamics. You know, we don't speak up about private ethical concerns because we think that ethics talk, or what I call here and now ethics talk, it's just out of bounds for the workplace. It doesn't belong in the workplace. We just don't like to talk that way. And of course, that's even besides the silencing power of power, where people are, of course, concerned about retribution and what would happen if they do bring up a concern that they might have.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a153"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts83" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts87">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>[overlap]Or my part. Yeah. Right. Or my part. How do I make it very personal? Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a154"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts91" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts93">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes, great. And this is where the research can really help us. So, in communication, what we will often do in order to demonstrate this is rely on experiments. And what you do is you put people in a situation, a scenario, and you see how they communicate. And so, imagine yourself in this kind of scenario. Let me, I'll give you a flavor for what that seems like.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a155"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts95" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts97">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Imagine that you're a bookkeeper of a company, okay? And your coworker asks you to write a check from company funds to reimburse them for their own personal computer. Now you know this request is unethical, it's a violation of the wage policy, there wouldn't even be discretionary funds after this for the rest of the month if you did it. Now respond to your coworker as though this were a real situation.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a156"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts101" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts104">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>And so we put people in such in</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a157"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts107" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts109">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Scenarios like that. And one of the key things that we do is we then ask them privately, okay, so was that request unethical? And we absolutely confirm that, yeah, that was unethical of my coworker to ask me. But then we go ahead and code what they say. Now, we now have several data sets of hundreds of working adults. Here's what we find: despite the fact that 100% of these working adults say that request was unethical, only a fraction, only 10%, ever deny the request on ethical grounds. I mean, they did not deny the request. They might say, oh, I can't do that, or there's an operational reason why I can't do that, or maybe there's a policy, but they don't bring up ethics. They don't say, I can't do that because that seems wrong or not right, or that doesn't seem, that would be against my integrity or my conscience. We don't talk that way because we've been socialized to say that that doesn't belong in the workplace. And that's a problem because if we can't talk that way, how can we think that way together? And so that's what's important.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a158"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts115" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts117">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, absolutely. So I think one of the big deals with the moral mom effect is the more we understand how pervasive this issue is in the workplace, that people might privately have a concern, but they don't publicly discuss it. And then we start to clock a little bit and we start thinking, well, what's the implication of that over time? Start to have some interesting realizations. Now, if people can't or won't talk ethics in the here and now, it'll be hard for them to catch ethical concerns or problems while those problems are still small and resolvable. And that can be a huge thing where if issues are allowed to go on, people might privately have concerns, but they look around and nobody else is talking that way. And then that might lead them to believe over time, oh, maybe I'm alone here. And maybe that's silly of me. This is just the way business is done. This is just the way it works. And so then you get that gradual incremental step down. Unfortunately, you get rationalizations, self-deceptions or group self-deceptions. And it's in other words, then you get ethical problems and private ethical concerns. They go unaddressed. They begin to fester and grow, unfortunately, until they become intractable. They become impossible to address.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a159"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts131" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts133">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>That's a common theme. Somebody knew.Yes</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a160"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts139" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts141">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, great. I mean, I think for the people who care about ethics in the workplace, leaders and aspiring leaders out there, I want you to not just lead by example. I want you to speak by example. Use language like integrity and ethics, right and wrong. And go ahead and use that language. The research is showing that when bosses use that kind of plain but ethically charged language, it helps everybody else say, oh, okay, I can talk that way. And it can be a really powerful way in which we speak by example. We say, this is what I care about. Talk to me about it because this is an important value to us. Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a161"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts147" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts149">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, yeah. I think one of the reasons that people avoid ethics talk is because it might feel like too fuzzy, philosophical, or religious. And the fact of the matter is, not everybody needs to be a philosopher. We can all just take a deep breath here. There really are ethical dilemmas and puzzles, but for the most part, we know we shouldn't steal. We know we shouldn't harm people. And for the most part, a lot of us agree with that. So just be comfortable and confident in that fact. And what we want to do is we want to focus on the realization that for the most part, people have private ethical judgments. They already have them.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a162"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts151" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts153">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>They're already there. And so, can we just have the conversation, be mindful, and try to adapt our work and pursue ethical excellence. Work towards moving the conversation from is this right or wrong only to how can we be better today? How can we keep doing work with increasing amount of integrity? And something we can be proud of. So, we're going to avoid that philosophical language where possible because we want to hear from people and we don't want to end up excluding voices when people feel like, oh, I can't really participate in high philosophy discourse with you. Say, hey, don't worry about that. Let's just tell me what you think and where are you? And then let's have the conversation. Yeah.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a163"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts159" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts161">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, this is sort of an interesting realization that the research is pointing to. And that's when, and it's related to the first two really, when we make a habit of talking about the body and what the body knows, for example, hey, follow your heart, or what's our heart telling us here? What's our gut? What's our gut telling us here? What do we know to be true in our bones? What do we know to be really valuable in our bones? All of those kinds of uses, descriptions of the body, believe it or not, helps people feel more calm. and more willing to have here and now ethics talk because I think what's happening there is they do trust those some of those signals they're getting from their body and they feel like they can reference those maybe easier than they can engage in some sort of sort of philosophical discourse or religious debate. That's not where we need to go necessarily. We can actually do have a lot of conversation where we just sort of reference, hey, this is the way I'm feeling and it's going to open some of those doors for us to be able to have an authentic moral dialogue.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a164"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts175" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts177">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, for leaders and aspiring leaders out there, I just want to I want to encourage you to find examples of times in your past or in the team's past where somebody spoke up and really helped you, really helped you all pursue ethical excellence, helped you pursue work with a lot of integrity, and tell those stories. They can be quick little stories, little reminders, and that begins to put people on notice that that's what we're looking for. That's really what we want. We don't want yes men and yes women. We want people who can be help us be moral barometers as we move through the complexity of work life sometimes. Organizational communication scholar, Dr. Jeff Cassing, he reminds us of an organization who described their, quote, guardian angels. And you would get guardian angel status if you were somebody who spoke up when the time was right with something we really needed to hear. And I think that's a wonderful way in which leaders can demonstrate that this is what they value. This is the kind of talk that they want from people.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a165"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts183" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts185">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, I think this is really the backbone to all of it. When we build trusting relationships, that's really what helps people have the courage to talk this way and feel safe about talking this way. And we need to build those trusting relationships so that we can, you know, we need to be humble and patient and loving towards people, but we need to also have this conversation because the alternative is silence. And that silence can be the source of a lot of festering problems. And so as we build trusting relationships, I mean, what does that look like? Sometimes that looks like just remembering people's kids' names or their grandkids' names, asking about their hobbies. Not, you know, not five days out of five days a week, but enough to let people know, hey, we're approachable. We do care about you as an individual. And you can trust us. You can trust me to speak up and tell me what you really think. So building trusting relationships really is such a key. It's a foundation to all of this.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a166"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts199" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts204">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Had a great time. And until the next time, we'll see you all later. Take care.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
    </TIER>
